{"name":"Blustery Wind","hex":"#b6c5c1","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blustery","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Blowing in loud and abrupt bursts.","Accompanied by strong wind."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blustery"},{"term":"wind","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Real or perceived movement of atmospheric air usually caused by convection or differences in air pressure.","Air artificially put in motion by any force or action."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To blow air through a wind instrument or horn to make a sound.","To cause (someone) to become breathless, as by a blow to the abdomen, or by physical exertion, running, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wind"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wind","blocks":[{"text":"Wind is the natural movement of air or other gases relative to a planet's surface. Winds occur on a range of scales, from thunderstorm flows lasting tens of minutes, to local breezes generated by heating of land surfaces and lasting a few hours, to global winds resulting from the difference in absorption of solar energy between the climate zones on Earth. The study of wind is known as anemology."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif/330px-Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind","wikidata":"Q8094"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/10_PM_March_12_surface_analysis_of_Great_Blizzard_of_1888.png/500px-10_PM_March_12_surface_analysis_of_Great_Blizzard_of_1888.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/10_PM_March_12_surface_analysis_of_Great_Blizzard_of_1888.png/500px-10_PM_March_12_surface_analysis_of_Great_Blizzard_of_1888.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Surface analysis of the Great Blizzard of 1888. Areas with greater isobaric packing indicate higher winds.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A10_PM_March_12_surface_analysis_of_Great_Blizzard_of_1888.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Anemometer_2745.JPG/500px-Anemometer_2745.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Anemometer_2745.JPG/1280px-Anemometer_2745.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Anemometer_2745.JPG/500px-Anemometer_2745.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cup-type anemometer on a remote meteorological station","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAnemometer_2745.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Wind_barbs.gif/330px-Wind_barbs.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Wind_barbs.gif/330px-Wind_barbs.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wind plotting within a station model","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWind_barbs.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Earth_Global_Circulation.jpg/500px-Earth_Global_Circulation.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Earth_Global_Circulation.jpg/500px-Earth_Global_Circulation.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Winds are part of Earth's atmospheric circulation","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEarth_Global_Circulation.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/comm